= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Apply the same change of <reciprocal lattice> coordinates to the <phonon> <wave vectors>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf q_{P_1}:\ (1,0)_r\mapsto(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)_p,\qquad\mathbf q_{P_2}:\ (0,0)_r\mapsto(0,0)_p.}
$$
Although $(1,0)_r$ is an integer coordinate of the rectangular reciprocal grid, it is not a <reciprocal lattice vector> of the actual primitive crystal. Thus $P_1$ is not equivalent to the <Brillouin zone> centre.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For ordinary one-phonon <infrared> <optical absorption>, both an energy and a momentum selection rule must hold. The optical <photon> has negligible <crystal momentum> on the scale of the <Brillouin zone>, so <crystal momentum conservation> selects $\mathbf q\simeq0$ modulo a genuine <reciprocal lattice vector>. In addition, the mode must change the macroscopic <electric polarization>, with a component along the incident <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>.

In terms of atomic displacements $\mathbf u_j$ and <Born effective charge> tensors $Z_j^*$, the relevant derivative is proportional to $\sum_jZ_j^*\mathbf u_j$ per <primitive unit cell>. Thus an <optical phonon> at $\mathbf q=0$ is not automatically infrared active: its dipole contributions may cancel. Disorder-assisted and multiphonon processes relax these simple first-order selection rules.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

$P_1$ has primitive coordinates $(1/2,1/2)$ and therefore fails the first-order optical momentum condition; its local $V_1$ pattern also has zero dipole derivative. $P_2$ is a zone-centre mode with the dipole-active $V_2$ pattern, so its molecular dipole changes add coherently.
$$
\boxed{P_1\text{ is IR inactive};\qquad P_2\text{ is IR active}.}
$$
The allowed absorption for $P_2$ uses an <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> with an out-of-plane component. At normal incidence on an ideal free-standing layer the electric field is in the plane, so that specific measurement geometry would suppress it despite the mode's intrinsic <vibrational infrared activity>.

